WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:02.940
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.300
This is the second of my videos on the Doctor
Strange post-credits scenes.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.709
In my first video, I discussed the mid-credits
scene from the film which teases a major plot

00:00:10.709 --> 00:00:15.420
point from an upcoming Marvel Phase 3 movie,
but in this video I'm going to discuss the

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.940
final post-credits scene that appears right
at the end of the movie after all the credits

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:20.940
have rolled.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.039
Just so you're aware, if you haven't seen
Doctor Strange yet, there is a major spoiler

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:29.119
here for a future movie, so if you want to
avoid that you can check out my spoiler-free

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:30.900
review of the film right here.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.310
The scene opens with a hooded figure walking
into a workshop where Jonathan Pangborn is

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.310
working.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:40.090
Pangborn asks "can I help you?" and the hooded
figure takes off his hood, revealing himself

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.090
as Mordo.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.960
Mordo replies: "I carried you to Kamar-Taj
on a stretcher.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.660
Look at you now Pangborn."

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.780
Just to recap: Pangborn is the paralysed man
who Doctor Strange refused to meet when he

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.680
was a neurosurgeon as he said he was untreatable.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.350
However, after his own accident, Strange met
with Pangborn and learned that Pangborn had

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.460
discovered the power of magic at Kamar-Taj
and was using it to heal himself so he could

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.480
live his life without being paralysed.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.759
As for Mordo, he starts the film as Doctor
Strange's ally, but by the end of the movie

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:10.689
is furious at Strange for using the Eye of
Agamotto to manipulate time.

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:15.039
And on top of that, Mordo is also angry because
he discovered that the Ancient One was using

00:01:15.039 --> 00:01:18.820
power from the Dark Dimension to live longer
than would normally be possible for a human

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:19.820
being.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.130
Ok, back to the post-credits scene: Pangborn
senses there's something wrong about Mordo,

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.470
so reaches for a crowbar.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.760
He asks Mordo what he can do for him.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.909
Mordo explains that he's been away for several
months and had a revelation that "the true

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:37.079
purpose of a sorcerer is to twist things out
of their proper shape, stealing power, perverting

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:38.740
nature, like you."

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.359
Pangborn replies that he's stolen nothing
and that the power he holds is his own.

00:01:42.359 --> 00:01:45.420
Mordo retorts ominously that "Power has a
purpose."

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.439
Pangborn, who now realises Mordo has evil
intent, tries to attack him with the crowbar,

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:55.170
but Mordo reaches into Pangborn's stomach
and extracts a yellow light causing Pangborn

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.649
to collapse, his body now shaking as he's
lost the magic he used to fight his paralysis.

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:03.179
Mordo then says he now understands what is
wrong with the world, which is that there

00:02:03.179 --> 00:02:04.790
are too many sorcerers.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:08.149
The scene ends and cuts to the words "Doctor
Strange will return."

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:13.180
So, this scene sets up a possible Doctor Strange
sequel and a villainous role for Mordo in

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.670
the future MCU.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:18.879
In the comics, Mordo studied with the Ancient
One, but had nefarious objectives, and in

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:22.700
this final post-credits scene, we get to see
his character tie up with his traditional

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.680
depiction as a villain in the comics.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.739
In a Doctor Strange sequel, some of the plot
could revolve around Mordo trying to rid the

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:32.900
world of sorcerers, for example, by taking
their powers like he did with Pangborn and

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.980
amassing them for himself, getting ever more
powerful.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.760
Or what about this for a potential sequel
idea involving Mordo?

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.099
Strange obviously succeeded in angering Dormammu
in the movie by trapping him in a time loop

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:48.549
and forcing him to agree to end his assault
on Strange's world and never come back.

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:53.020
Well, Strange also drew the wrath of Dormammu
in the Strange Tales comics when Doctor Strange

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:57.560
told Dormammu: 'you must vow never to invade
the earth'; and Dormammu agreed though he

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.439
was determined to get revenge on Strange.

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:04.019
Then later in the comics, Dormammu got around
that agreement by using Mordo as a proxy,

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:09.019
telling Mordo, 'I shall give you power without
limits so you may destroy Doctor Strange'.

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:13.790
So, perhaps we'll see Mordo ally with Dormammu
in a similar way in a Doctor Strange movie

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:14.790
sequel.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:18.730
Or Mordo could come back before then, for
instance, in Avengers: Infinity War, if he

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.969
gets recruited by Thanos to help him recover
the Infinity Stones.

00:03:21.969 --> 00:03:26.049
By the way, I also felt there was an interesting,
unintended, parallel between the post-credits

00:03:26.049 --> 00:03:29.879
scene in Doctor Strange and the mid-credits
scene in the Green Lantern movie where Mark

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:34.030
Strong's Sinestro, who's previously been on
the Green Lantern's side, takes off his green

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.099
ring and puts on the yellow ring signalling
his transformation into a villain, as well

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:41.150
as setting up a potential Green Lantern sequel
that never was.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.920
So, that was the post-credits scene for Doctor
Strange.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.939
Were you excited to see Mordo turn to the
dark side and fulfil his comic-book villain

00:03:47.939 --> 00:03:48.939
role?

00:03:48.939 --> 00:03:51.260
And what would you like to see in a Doctor
Strange sequel?

